{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CggZhPkofqwfHJmYvoA8w","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Result: Dq ≈ 523 cm⁻¹ (Δt = 10Dq ≈ 5 230 cm⁻¹) and B ≈ 700 cm⁻¹.** I worked this by hand and did not use the tools.\n\n**Approach.** Tetrahedral d⁷ has a ⁴A₂ ground term. Its spin-allowed bands are ν₁ = ⁴T₂, ν₂ = ⁴T₁(F) and ν₃ = ⁴T₁(P). I treated this as the octahedral d³ problem, because the two share the same term structure. Here Dq is the tetrahedral value, so Δt = 10Dq.\n\n**Equations.** Energies are measured from the ⁴A₂ ground term, which lies at −12Dq above the ⁴F barycentre.\n- ν₁ = 10Dq\n- ν₂ and ν₃ are the eigenvalues of the ⁴T₁ matrix [[6Dq, 6B],[6B, 15B]], shifted by +12Dq.\n- The sum is ν₂ + ν₃ = 30Dq + 15B.\n- Each eigenvalue ν satisfies (18Dq − ν)(15B + 12Dq − ν) = 36B².\n\n**Solution.**\n1. The sum rule gives 30Dq + 15B = 26 160 cm⁻¹, so B = 1744 − 2Dq.\n2. Substituting this into the secular equation with ν = 7 520 cm⁻¹ and solving numerically gives Dq ≈ 523 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 698 cm⁻¹.\n\n**Check against ν₁.** This fit predicts ν₁ = 10Dq ≈ 5 230 cm⁻¹, while the observed band is at 4 330 cm⁻¹.\n- If I instead fix 10Dq at 4 330 cm⁻¹, the sum rule gives B ≈ 878 cm⁻¹. That value reproduces ν₂ and ν₃ very poorly.\n- The 4 330 cm⁻¹ band is therefore not consistent with the simple three-parameter model. This is common for tetrahedral Co(II), where the ⁴T₂ band is low, broad and sensitive to spin–orbit coupling and low-symmetry effects.\n- I used ν₂ and ν₃ because they fix B well. Treat Dq as uncertain by about ±100 cm⁻¹.\n\n**Covalency.** B ≈ 700 cm⁻¹ is about 72% of the free-ion value for Co²⁺ (≈ 970 cm⁻¹), so the nephelauxetic effect is moderate to strong.\n\n{\"Dq\": 523, \"B\": 698, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Fit of tetrahedral d7 (octahedral d3-analog) to ν2 and ν3; ν1 is predicted at ~5230 vs 4330 observed, so Dq is only approximate.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reaso